ockquote><div><br></div><div>=C2=A0</div><blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" =
style=3D"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);pa=
dding-left:1ex"> <br>
&gt; *=C2=A0 In TLS 1.3, that behaviour has changed<br>
&gt; * so the *implicit* meaning of &quot;server sends TLS finished&quot; i=
s no longer &quot;server has seen client cert&quot;<br>
&gt; *=C2=A0 Since EAP-TLS wishes to authenticate the client cert, it MUST =
have an EAP state after the &quot;TLS Finished&quot; message<br>
&gt; *=C2=A0 Since EAP-TLS does not provide for any data exchanged in the E=
AP layer, any data MUST be mangled into TLS<br>
&gt; * and the only possibility is TLS application data.<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt;=C2=A0 =C2=A0I&#39;d like to know if any of these assumptions are false=
.<br>
<br>
(I cannot contradict any of those assumptions, though I don&#39;t think I w=
ould<br>
be one of the likely candidates to do so.)</blockquote><div><br></div><div>=
<div><br></div><div>[Joe] Based on RFC 5216 the server could fail the finis=
hed message or as section 2.1.3 shows it could send the finish and then it =
can send an=C2=A0Alert and result in EAP-Failure.=C2=A0 In this case it wou=
ld be possible for an attacker to remove the Alert and send a success.=C2=
=A0 Whether your implementation ends up in this scenario or not probably de=
pends upon why the auth failed and the behavior of your TLS library.</div><=
/div><div><br></div><div>I do not believe that RFC 5216 provides protected =
result indications.=C2=A0 =C2=A0It would be a fine thing to add, but it is =
